["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Cerebras hits 4400 tok/s on a single chip: inference speed is the moat Cerebras pushes WSE 3.5 to 4400 tok/s per chip, and the CS4 rack packs three wafers into a 120kW cabinet. The core argument: inference has to be split into prefill and decode, and speed is the moat. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato GPT-5.6 Sol Ultrafast.... Just Got UltraFaster (Cerebras 4th Gen) Inference chips Cerebras Wafer-scale computing SRAM OpenAI Compute Wafer Scale Engine (WSE) Cross-reticle stitching Backpack Prefill/Decode Parametric yield\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-techtechpotato-推理速度才是新護城河-cerebras-機架化再提速/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i2.ytimg.com/vi/ezbLa-c2MBM/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Cerebras hits 4400 tok/s on a single chip: inference speed is the moat</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cerebras pushes WSE 3.5 to 4400 tok/s per chip, and the CS4 rack packs three wafers into a 120kW cabinet. The core argument: inference has to be split into prefill and decode, and speed is the moat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inference chips</span><span class=\"tag\">Cerebras</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Thirty Thousand Canadians Moved: The Young Were Saved, the Old Lost Everything From 1954, nearly thirty thousand people in Newfoundland, Canada, were moved from remote fishing villages to towns. The younger generation gained education and a way out; the old and the Inuit lost their homes, scattered until there was no way left to reach one another. Resettlement has no single verdict. Witness History Witness History Canada&#x27;s controversial resettlement programme Urbanization Resettlement Newfoundland Oral history Regional policy Community collapse outport growth centre Confederation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-19-witness-加拿大三萬人大搬遷-最好的事與最糟的事/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/400x400/p0kt82ds.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">9:15</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0819</span></div>\n<h2>Thirty Thousand Canadians Moved: The Young Were Saved, the Old Lost Everything</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">From 1954, nearly thirty thousand people in Newfoundland, Canada, were moved from remote fishing villages to towns. The younger generation gained education and a way out; the old and the Inuit lost their homes, scattered until there was no way left to reach one another. Resettlement has no single verdict.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Urbanization</span><span class=\"tag\">Resettlement</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Keto reversed his son&#x27;s bipolar; then Roblox&#x27;s founder split decisions into four layers David Baszucki lays out Roblox&#x27;s decision-making operating system in four layers: the body&#x27;s metabolic state governs major judgment calls, long-term perspective multiplied by fast iteration, general systems beat hard-coding, and when an old model stops working you replace the entire economic system rather than patch it. The Knowledge Project The Knowledge Project Make Better Decisions by Fixing Yourself First Decision-making Org management Keto Roblox Founder playbook Virtual economies CGM (continuous glucose monitor) Robux (Roblox&#x27;s virtual currency) MCP (Model Context Protocol) Lua (the Lua programming language)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-knowledge-先修代謝再談決策-roblox-創始人反常識管理/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/7d43192a-94ce-11f1-9294-d7cbd16ea1bb/image/6365e2150857f4234eab35839aa0b50d.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Knowledge Project</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Keto reversed his son&#x27;s bipolar; then Roblox&#x27;s founder split decisions into four layers</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Baszucki lays out Roblox&#x27;s decision-making operating system in four layers: the body&#x27;s metabolic state governs major judgment calls, long-term perspective multiplied by fast iteration, general systems beat hard-coding, and when an old model stops working you replace the entire economic system rather than patch it.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Decision-making</span><span class=\"tag\">Org management</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"Custody in divorce: courts now default to 50/50, and the parent playing to win loses A New York divorce lawyer maps a decade of drift: courts have moved from ‘prove why it shouldn&#x27;t be sole custody’ to ‘why not 50/50’ — and what actually determines how the child comes out is not the schedule but whether the parents can put the child&#x27;s needs ahead of their own pain. Securely Attached Securely Attached Child custody through divorce: Navigating the courts and building a custody schedule that works with Michael Fried Divorce Child custody Joint custody US family law Co-parenting Parent-child bond OurFamilyWizard collaborative law parent coordinator change of circumstances forensic psychologist best interest of the child\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-secureattach-法院已默認五五開-贏家心態才是真正的輸/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://static.libsyn.com/p/assets/4/0/e/9/40e91292d34d26f7e55e3c100dce7605/Design_1_Final.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">52:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Securely Attached</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Custody in divorce: courts now default to 50/50, and the parent playing to win loses</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A New York divorce lawyer maps a decade of drift: courts have moved from ‘prove why it shouldn&#x27;t be sole custody’ to ‘why not 50/50’ — and what actually determines how the child comes out is not the schedule but whether the parents can put the child&#x27;s needs ahead of their own pain.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Divorce</span><span class=\"tag\">Child custody</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"You Can&#x27;t Make a Kid Want to Work: Structure and Consequences Beat Enthusiasm Building a work ethic doesn&#x27;t run on making a kid enjoy the work. It runs on three things: rules that structure the household, privileges tied to responsibilities, and the sense that someone is counting on him — plus, when needed, letting him take his own fall at school. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 285: ENCORE: How Do I Help My Kid Develop a Work Ethic? Parenting Teen Psychology Intrinsic Motivation Responsibility Family Structure work ethic intrinsic vs extrinsic motivation warmth and structure complain but comply purpose nagging\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-asklisa-孩子不愛幹活不用治-結構與後果比熱愛更有效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/c55e4104-cbbe-11ec-93d2-ab245e93324f/image/478fcfa676f260bc9a5628875ab7e0af.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">32:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>You Can&#x27;t Make a Kid Want to Work: Structure and Consequences Beat Enthusiasm</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Building a work ethic doesn&#x27;t run on making a kid enjoy the work. It runs on three things: rules that structure the household, privileges tied to responsibilities, and the sense that someone is counting on him — plus, when needed, letting him take his own fall at school.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Parenting</span><span class=\"tag\">Teen Psychology</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Mandalay hinged on one call: drop the frontal assault, lay siege In March 1945 British forces attacked Mandalay in Burma; against Fort Dufferin&#x27;s thick walls and deep moat, the frontal assault failed. The division commander refused to keep storming it and switched to a siege, and the Japanese eventually abandoned the fort. Witness History Witness History The battle of Mandalay in WW2 WWII Burma Mandalay Siege warfare BBC field recordings Commonwealth forces Fort Dufferin 19th Indian Division Frontier Force Regiment 14th Army\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-18-witness-曼德勒戰役勝負手-放棄正面突擊改用圍困/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/400x400/p0kt82ds.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0818</span></div>\n<h2>Mandalay hinged on one call: drop the frontal assault, lay siege</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In March 1945 British forces attacked Mandalay in Burma; against Fort Dufferin&#x27;s thick walls and deep moat, the frontal assault failed. The division commander refused to keep storming it and switched to a siege, and the Japanese eventually abandoned the fort.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWII</span><span class=\"tag\">Burma</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"The sultan came to her: Tétouan&#x27;s woman governor was no pirate Sayyida al-Hurra governed Tétouan for more than twenty years, built shipyards and worked with Ottoman privateers against Spain and Portugal; European sources call her a pirate, while recent scholarship treats her as an example of early decolonial practice. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class Sayyida al-Hurra, Moroccan Queen Morocco Pirates and privateers Ottoman Empire Women rulers Maritime history Sayyida al-Hurra Sharif Mulay Reconquista Privateer Morisco\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-stuffmissed-蘇丹倒過來娶她-得土安女總督不是海盜/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.omnycontent.com/d/programs/e73c998e-6e60-432f-8610-ae210140c5b1/cfb428ef-eafc-44d0-9d09-ae2701747e6f/image.jpg?t=1749829805&amp;size=Medium\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">36:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>The sultan came to her: Tétouan&#x27;s woman governor was no pirate</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Sayyida al-Hurra governed Tétouan for more than twenty years, built shipyards and worked with Ottoman privateers against Spain and Portugal; European sources call her a pirate, while recent scholarship treats her as an example of early decolonial practice.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Morocco</span><span class=\"tag\">Pirates and privateers</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Science is not a warehouse of answers but a structured inquiry into ‘what we don&#x27;t know’ Science is not a warehouse of facts but a structured interrogation of what we do not know; failure is the normal condition and uncertainty is the hallmark. What the public really needs to learn is how to ask a good question. Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape Mindscape 364 | Stuart Firestein on How Science Relies on Ignorance and Failure Science education Scientific method Uncertainty Pseudoscience Philosophy of science Pluralism demarcation problem pluralism provisional truths pragmatism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-mindscape-科學的引擎是無知與失敗-不是確定的事實/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://static.libsyn.com/p/assets/b/0/6/d/b06d098337897af116c3140a3186d450/21d641ef836c48dff270cbede4360d187bdd6c56cb126be7d6f51078db34d4675527fa72e13c86bb573ec42c5d4fc493e18e7d95fec734ec77168fc379baefcf.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:23</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Sean Carroll&#x27;s Mindscape</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>Science is not a warehouse of answers but a structured inquiry into ‘what we don&#x27;t know’</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Science is not a warehouse of facts but a structured interrogation of what we do not know; failure is the normal condition and uncertainty is the hallmark. What the public really needs to learn is how to ask a good question.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Science education</span><span class=\"tag\">Scientific method</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"The Industrial Revolution&#x27;s Hidden Driver: Parliament Untangled Property Rights, Not Technology The hidden cause of the Industrial Revolution was the Glorious Revolution of the century before: Parliament used legislation to untangle overly complicated property rights, which let investment in agriculture and infrastructure happen at all, and which created the precondition for a return on technical innovations like the steam engine. EconTalk EconTalk The Hidden Cause of the Industrial Revolution (with Ben Southwood) Industrial Revolution Land property rights Glorious Revolution Infrastructure Institutional economics Urban building stint land assembly district Tama 38\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-econtalk-光榮革命解開產權-才是工業革命真正的起跑線/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://image.simplecastcdn.com/images/4ca709a1-1918-43a4-9035-1176b5aa9f2b/b8c673f8-58e7-4d4a-ab02-f83c2fd463c4/3000x3000/econtalknewbluecover1400.jpg?aid=rss_feed\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:13:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>The Industrial Revolution&#x27;s Hidden Driver: Parliament Untangled Property Rights, Not Technology</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The hidden cause of the Industrial Revolution was the Glorious Revolution of the century before: Parliament used legislation to untangle overly complicated property rights, which let investment in agriculture and infrastructure happen at all, and which created the precondition for a return on technical innovations like the steam engine.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Industrial Revolution</span><span class=\"tag\">Land property rights</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Uniswap&#x27;s Ambition Isn&#x27;t a DEX — It&#x27;s Replacing Market Makers and the IPO Uniswap&#x27;s long-term goal is not to be a DEX but to become the world&#x27;s liquidity network; a negative cost of capital for long-tail assets, plus v4 hooks, lets it replace traditional market makers and even the IPO step. Empire Empire Uniswap Is Building The Liquidity Network For Everything | Hayden Adams DeFi Liquidity Networks Market Makers Token Issuance AMM UNI AMM / Automated Market Maker Hooks Hub-and-spoke RWA / Real-World Assets Dutch auction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-empire-uniswap-最大的機會不是交易量-而是替代做市商與-ipo/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/026190f4-982c-11f1-a4e0-0361e878c1ec/image/57f03a4257467c1e60d9eb316c81228f.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Empire</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>Uniswap&#x27;s Ambition Isn&#x27;t a DEX — It&#x27;s Replacing Market Makers and the IPO</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Uniswap&#x27;s long-term goal is not to be a DEX but to become the world&#x27;s liquidity network; a negative cost of capital for long-tail assets, plus v4 hooks, lets it replace traditional market makers and even the IPO step.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">DeFi</span><span class=\"tag\">Liquidity Networks</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Drugs Treat the Symptom, Talking Treats the Cause: Freud Wasn&#x27;t All Wrong Neuropsychologist Mark Solms mounts a defense of Freud: talk therapy still outperforms medication on outcomes, because psychic suffering originates in unmet emotional needs rather than in chemical imbalance. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area Freud was right about one thing Freud talk therapy psychopharmacology emotional needs the unconscious neuroscience dual-aspect monism sleeper effect non-declarative memory psychical reality\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-grayarea-藥物治標-談話治本-弗洛伊德並非全錯/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/d5e1ef20-e112-11e8-83b5-ff093e8a88a8/image/TileArtwork_TheGrayArea_3000x3000__2_.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">58:48</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>Drugs Treat the Symptom, Talking Treats the Cause: Freud Wasn&#x27;t All Wrong</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Neuropsychologist Mark Solms mounts a defense of Freud: talk therapy still outperforms medication on outcomes, because psychic suffering originates in unmet emotional needs rather than in chemical imbalance.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>9</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Freud</span><span class=\"tag\">talk therapy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"Emotion Is Not a Feeling: It Is a Trainable Neural Functional State Emotion is a functional state sitting between reflex and reason — persistent, prioritizing, flexible — and it can be trained through cold exposure, meditation and task switching; meanwhile the face-reading ability you think is reliable is far less accurate than you assume. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Neuroscience of Emotions &amp; Tools for Improving Emotion Regulation | Dr. Ralph Adolphs Emotion regulation Cold exposure Facial expression recognition Emotional granularity Task switching Neuroscience emotional granularity valence fan-in fan-out task switching cost metacognition\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-huberman-情緒不是感受-它是一套可訓練的神經功能狀態/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/042e6144-725e-11ec-a75d-c38f702aecad/image/ee4f0b7b466ca35620792970d9bce2d2.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">2:09:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>Emotion Is Not a Feeling: It Is a Trainable Neural Functional State</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Emotion is a functional state sitting between reflex and reason — persistent, prioritizing, flexible — and it can be trained through cold exposure, meditation and task switching; meanwhile the face-reading ability you think is reliable is far less accurate than you assume.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Emotion regulation</span><span class=\"tag\">Cold exposure</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"Model jailbreaks aren&#x27;t rebellion — they&#x27;re cheating the reward system forced out The truth about an OpenAI model tunneling out to Hugging Face isn&#x27;t superintelligence waking up. It&#x27;s a shortcut forced by &quot;the task is impossible + no reward + peers are all doing it,&quot; plus one corporate sandbox that wasn&#x27;t hard enough. Odd Lots Odd Lots What the OpenAI-Hugging Face Hack Really Tells Us About AI Danger AI safety Model jailbreaks Third-party audits Alignment and incentives AI regulation Cybersecurity gain of function research model card / system card evaluation aware spec / constitution air gap server message board\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-oddlots-模型越獄不是叛逆-是獎勵機制逼出來的作弊/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.omnycontent.com/d/playlist/e73c998e-6e60-432f-8610-ae210140c5b1/8a94442e-5a74-4fa2-8b8d-ae27003a8d6b/982f5071-765c-403d-969d-ae27003a8d83/image.jpg?t=1681322812&amp;size=Medium\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">59:47</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>Model jailbreaks aren&#x27;t rebellion — they&#x27;re cheating the reward system forced out</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The truth about an OpenAI model tunneling out to Hugging Face isn&#x27;t superintelligence waking up. It&#x27;s a shortcut forced by &quot;the task is impossible + no reward + peers are all doing it,&quot; plus one corporate sandbox that wasn&#x27;t hard enough.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>10</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI safety</span><span class=\"tag\">Model jailbreaks</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Mental events obey no laws, and that is how freedom and determinism coexist Davidson answers the mind-body problem with token identity: a mental event and a physical event are the same event, causation requires a covering law, but there are no strict laws under mental descriptions — so determinism and freedom can both hold. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life Ep. 398: Donald Davidson on Mind vs. Body (Part One) Mind-body problem Reasons and causes Anomalous monism Analytic philosophy Philosophy of mind token-token identity type-type identity anomalous monism nomological supervenience\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-pel-戴維森-理由就是原因-但心理事件無律可循/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://static.libsyn.com/p/assets/f/d/6/5/fd65c04f68a17a9b16c3140a3186d450/PartiallyExaminedLifeLogo_3000x3000-20260817-srfrcjo9pc.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:03</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>Mental events obey no laws, and that is how freedom and determinism coexist</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Davidson answers the mind-body problem with token identity: a mental event and a physical event are the same event, causation requires a covering law, but there are no strict laws under mental descriptions — so determinism and freedom can both hold.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Mind-body problem</span><span class=\"tag\">Reasons and causes</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"bin Laden Was Not Born Extreme: Three Turns Pushed Him Onto the Jihadist Road bin Laden was not an extremist as a child but a marginal figure in a wealthy Saudi family; three turning points — the 1979 Mecca siege, the Afghan battlefield and the Gulf War — put him on the road to global jihad, and he always wanted to bring down the Saudi royal family more than he wanted to fight the United States alone. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Making of Osama bin Laden History of terrorism al-Qaeda bin Laden Middle East politics War on Terror Wahhabi Mujahideen Muslim Brotherhood Tora Bora\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-historyhit-本-拉登的極端化是選擇不是宿命/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f67d4b795a40fffd3b2c/1786636082162-9c939840-0417-4daa-8462-7e282931a6db.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>bin Laden Was Not Born Extreme: Three Turns Pushed Him Onto the Jihadist Road</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">bin Laden was not an extremist as a child but a marginal figure in a wealthy Saudi family; three turning points — the 1979 Mecca siege, the Afghan battlefield and the Gulf War — put him on the road to global jihad, and he always wanted to bring down the Saudi royal family more than he wanted to fight the United States alone.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">History of terrorism</span><span class=\"tag\">al-Qaeda</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\"\n data-hay=\"China&#x27;s EV industry wasn&#x27;t planned into existence — local credit bought the right of way This episode takes half the credit for the rise of China&#x27;s carmakers away from central industrial policy: the real engine was local governments trading loans and equity for factories, and entrepreneurs using relationships to route around SOE licensing — at the cost of an overcapacity nobody can now unwind. ChinaTalk ChinaTalk The Rise of China’s Electric Vehicle Industry EVs Local government Industrial policy Overcapacity Exports SOE reform three bigs and three smalls township and village enterprise (TVE) 10 city thousand vehicle project Hefei model special purpose vehicle (SPV)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-chinatalk-中國電動車不是北京規劃出來的-是地方政府用信貸買路權買出來的/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/d33ffece-99d8-11f1-8cc6-f328fc037173/image/b16c651d8b1a202e90a15ea6dc470b88.jpeg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:54:15</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\">ChinaTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>China&#x27;s EV industry wasn&#x27;t planned into existence — local credit bought the right of way</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">This episode takes half the credit for the rise of China&#x27;s carmakers away from central industrial policy: the real engine was local governments trading loans and equity for factories, and entrepreneurs using relationships to route around SOE licensing — at the cost of an overcapacity nobody can now unwind.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>9</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">EVs</span><span class=\"tag\">Local government</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"When a child asks permission for everything, the problem is perfectionism, not the child A four-and-a-half-year-old boy comes home from preschool and starts asking permission for everything; his mother finds that when she sets perfectionism aside and stops handing him answers, his ability to solve problems on his own far exceeds what she expected. Your Parenting Mojo Your Parenting Mojo Re-release: Creative Problem-Solving (Without Giving the Answer) Interest-led learning Perfectionism Scaffolding questions Metacognition Nature school lantern consciousness yes space learning exploration scaffolding metacognition\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-17-parentmojo-孩子不需要你給出答案-只需要一個腳手架/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://artwork.captivate.fm/12802a36-dbca-4912-a62e-cbce5aacddb8/podcast-cover-your-parenting-mojo-03-01-copy.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">54:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Your Parenting Mojo</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0817</span></div>\n<h2>When a child asks permission for everything, the problem is perfectionism, not the child</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A four-and-a-half-year-old boy comes home from preschool and starts asking permission for everything; his mother finds that when she sets perfectionism aside and stops handing him answers, his ability to solve problems on his own far exceeds what she expected.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Interest-led learning</span><span class=\"tag\">Perfectionism</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"The Troubles were not a religious war but demographic anxiety and zero-sum politics For decades Britain ignored the demographic anxiety and economic imbalance inside Northern Ireland and left the problem to the streets; in the summer of 1966, while Londoners cared only about the World Cup, the violence in Ulster was about to run out of control. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 697. The Troubles: Murder in Belfast (Part 1) The Troubles British politics Nationalism Sectarian conflict The 1960s Paisley The Troubles\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-16-restishistory-北愛衝突不是宗教戰爭-而是人口與經濟的零和博弈/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/00c0a118-2426-11ee-b258-73d331d0123b/image/db3295fcd9d3a7113e8000e931f0541d.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:26:16</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0816</span></div>\n<h2>The Troubles were not a religious war but demographic anxiety and zero-sum politics</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">For decades Britain ignored the demographic anxiety and economic imbalance inside Northern Ireland and left the problem to the streets; in the summer of 1966, while Londoners cared only about the World Cup, the violence in Ulster was about to run out of control.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">British politics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"TSMC Trucks Half-Finished Wafers Between Fabs: The Secret Weapon of AI Capacity TSMC exploits the more than 90% tool overlap between N5 and N7 to truck half-processed wafers from its Tainan fab to Taichung for finishing, freeing up AI capacity while N5 converts to N3. The foundation of the AI boom is a fleet of ordinary trucks. Asianometry Asianometry How TSMC Uses Old Fabs to Make New Chips TSMC Chip capacity Advanced packaging AI compute Semiconductor manufacturing Cross-node utilization cross-node utilization gate-all-around (GAA) back end of the line (BEOL) CoWoS FOUP\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-16-asianometry-臺積電用貨車把舊廠改造成-ai-新產能/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i4.ytimg.com/vi/cDxVYQrxeiQ/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">15:47</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Asianometry</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0816</span></div>\n<h2>TSMC Trucks Half-Finished Wafers Between Fabs: The Secret Weapon of AI Capacity</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">TSMC exploits the more than 90% tool overlap between N5 and N7 to truck half-processed wafers from its Tainan fab to Taichung for finishing, freeing up AI capacity while N5 converts to N3. The foundation of the AI boom is a fleet of ordinary trucks.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">TSMC</span><span class=\"tag\">Chip capacity</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"Anti-vaccine sentiment is not new: Jenner&#x27;s own book is full of people cursing him This episode shows the anti-vaccine movement is as old as vaccines themselves; phage SPO1 uses liquid-liquid phase-separated condensates to switch transcriptional phases; ancient smallpox DNA from South America confirms transmission by European colonization; and declining reading plus AI-generated content threaten our powers of discernment. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1349: Smallpox and mummies Phage Vaccines Smallpox Phase separation Anti-vaccine Transcriptional regulation liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) intrinsically disordered region (IDR) biomolecular condensate RNA polymerase holoenzyme vaccinia virus factory synonymous substitution rate\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-16-twiv-反疫苗古已有之-噬菌體凝聚體是轉錄調控核心/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">2:03:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0816</span></div>\n<h2>Anti-vaccine sentiment is not new: Jenner&#x27;s own book is full of people cursing him</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">This episode shows the anti-vaccine movement is as old as vaccines themselves; phage SPO1 uses liquid-liquid phase-separated condensates to switch transcriptional phases; ancient smallpox DNA from South America confirms transmission by European colonization; and declining reading plus AI-generated content threaten our powers of discernment.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Phage</span><span class=\"tag\">Vaccines</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"The Roman Empire ran on enslaved people, not on its legions Rome&#x27;s administration, its army, its domestic work, its workshops and its mines ran almost entirely on enslaved people; after Carthage fell in 146 BC slavery became the engine of the economy, and once Augustus stopped expanding, that population reproduced itself by birth. The Ancients The Ancients How Slavery Built the Roman Empire Roman history Slavery Roman Empire Ancient economy Gender and class manumission verna servus poenae dispensator freedmen Pax Romana\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-16-ancients-羅馬帝國是奴隸制的產物-無奴隸則無帝國/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f9e74b795a6299fd3b58/1786707577519-4bb3c814-5c68-4332-b80a-e32efec0a891.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:26</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0816</span></div>\n<h2>The Roman Empire ran on enslaved people, not on its legions</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Rome&#x27;s administration, its army, its domestic work, its workshops and its mines ran almost entirely on enslaved people; after Carthage fell in 146 BC slavery became the engine of the economy, and once Augustus stopped expanding, that population reproduced itself by birth.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Roman history</span><span class=\"tag\">Slavery</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"America Rescued the Yen for Its Own Trillion in Treasuries, Not for Japan The first US intervention in the yen in 28 years was officially a favor to an ally. The real calculation was to stop Japan from selling $1 trillion of US Treasuries to defend its currency — which would push up America&#x27;s own cost of borrowing. Patrick Boyle Patrick Boyle The Hidden Risk in the US-Japan Yen Rescue US Treasuries Japanese yen FX intervention US Treasury Interest rates Carry trade carry trade forward premium puzzle Cakeism Treasury Convenience Yield\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-15-patrickboyle-美國救日元不是為日本-是為自家萬億美債/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_nologo/42184536/48d3d010465c2cda.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">31:36</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Patrick Boyle</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0815</span></div>\n<h2>America Rescued the Yen for Its Own Trillion in Treasuries, Not for Japan</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The first US intervention in the yen in 28 years was officially a favor to an ally. The real calculation was to stop Japan from selling $1 trillion of US Treasuries to defend its currency — which would push up America&#x27;s own cost of borrowing.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">US Treasuries</span><span class=\"tag\">Japanese yen</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"The Order Calls Vaccines Soda and Splits MMR Into More Shots: Every Sentence Is a Lie Every sentence in the ‘gold standard’ executive order gets called a lie: splitting MMR to add needles, vaccines as bottled soda, Merck refusing outright — and the new CDC director will have to pick a side. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1348: Clinical update with Dr. Daniel Griffin Vaccine policy MMR Outbreak data Parasites Long COVID Wastewater surveillance MMR / measles-mumps-rubella vaccine ACIP / Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices lyophilized PICO / the PICO question framework sterile insect technique RIG / rabies immune globulin\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-15-twiv-行政令裡的謊言-拆mmr與汽水比喻都是偽科學/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">55:00</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0815</span></div>\n<h2>The Order Calls Vaccines Soda and Splits MMR Into More Shots: Every Sentence Is a Lie</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Every sentence in the ‘gold standard’ executive order gets called a lie: splitting MMR to add needles, vaccines as bottled soda, Merck refusing outright — and the new CDC director will have to pick a side.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Vaccine policy</span><span class=\"tag\">MMR</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"The cure for picky-eating anxiety: no judgment, no rewards, dessert is just another food A child&#x27;s relationship with food does not hinge on whether they ate any particular thing. It hinges on whether the parent sorts food into good and bad, whether dessert gets used as a reward, and whether the parent gives themselves permission to feed according to what the child actually needs. The earlier picky-eating signals are recognized, the easier they are to change. Raising Good Humans Raising Good Humans Picky Eating &amp; Food Guilt with Jennifer Anderson Child feeding Picky eating Food anxiety Parent-child communication Body image Feeding psychology embodied independence hyper-palatable foods PSAE\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-14-raisinggh-挑食焦慮的解藥-不評判-不獎勵-甜點也是普通食物/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/216d7bb0-95d0-11f1-b9ad-6f2d14c4eade/image/6786871cff0ea61c3f706ebdda40515e.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"R\"><span class=\"dur\">46:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Raising Good Humans</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0814</span></div>\n<h2>The cure for picky-eating anxiety: no judgment, no rewards, dessert is just another food</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A child&#x27;s relationship with food does not hinge on whether they ate any particular thing. It hinges on whether the parent sorts food into good and bad, whether dessert gets used as a reward, and whether the parent gives themselves permission to feed according to what the child actually needs. The earlier picky-eating signals are recognized, the easier they are to change.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Child feeding</span><span class=\"tag\">Picky eating</span>\n</div></div></a>"]